  "notes": [
    "D3's harmlessness is a property of the tool, not of the hit: an identical argument list that names no identity cannot return a different answer to a different tenant, and the catalog tools are shown to return identical observations for identical arguments across tenants before any write.",
    "The reduction is one-sided: a hit counted damaging here is damaging, a hit called harmless is harmless only in the sense that the served value equals the value the tool would have produced. Both claims are auditable in policies[].by_tool and identity_dependence.tools.",
    "P3's identity damage is all get_user_details: its key drops user_id, so tenant B's profile read is answered with tenant A's profile. P1/P2 cannot do this, because their keys include the identity argument -- which is why their identity class is entirely harmless and P3's is not.",
    "The exact bound is per denominator, and the denominators differ in meaning: per admitted read is a call-level error rate, per served hit is the 'how much of what it served could be wrong' rate. Both are reported rather than one being chosen.",
    "payload_user_specific is not the verdict. get_order_details and get_reservation_details return payloads that name a user (the order's owner) and find_user_id_by_name_zip returns a user id, yet all three are identity-independent: the answer is selected by non-identity arguments, so a key that includes those arguments cannot confuse two people. The task's second clause -- takes user_id/email and returns user-specific data -- is realised as E1, which asks whether the payload changes when only the identity argument changes.",
    "The reduction, spelled out: P1's committed 344 = 240 warranted + 104 harmless; P2's 165 = 61 + 104; P3's 595 = 491 + 104. The harmless 104 is the same hit set in all three policies -- the catalog and flight reads -- so it is one measured quantity seen three times, not three independent findings.",
    "Clustered intervals stay slightly optimistic: each run keeps its own run-scope cache, so an instance's outcomes also depend on the instances before it in the same run. Four runs cannot support a block bootstrap, so that carryover is stated, not modelled."
  ],
